<p>Picture two households. In the first, someone steps out of the shower onto a bath mat that's damp in a way it shouldn't be. The cat has used her box for six years without a slip, and now there's a puddle on the softest surface in the room. In the second, there's a sharp smell by the back door and a dried streak running down the wall, about a hand's height off the floor. That cat still uses his litter box every day.</p>

<p>Both owners would say the same thing: "my cat is peeing outside the litter box." But these are usually two different behaviors with different causes, and the fix for one does little for the other. The first cat is most likely avoiding her box, often because something hurts or the box has become unpleasant. The second cat is marking, which is communication rather than a toilet problem.</p>

<p>You're in good company. The ASPCA estimates that at least 10% of cats develop elimination problems at some point. Rather than a flat list of causes, this guide works through the questions in the order a vet or behaviorist would, starting with the one that can't wait.</p>

<h2>First Check: Is This an Emergency?</h2>

<p><strong>If your cat, especially a male cat, is straining in the litter box again and again and producing only a few drops of urine or none at all, treat it as a medical emergency. Take him to your vet or the nearest emergency vet right away. Do not wait to see if it passes.</strong></p>

<p>That pattern can mean a urethral obstruction: the tube that carries urine out of the body is blocked by a stone or by a urethral plug, a soft mass of minerals, cells and mucus-like protein. Male cats are at greater risk because their urethra is longer and narrower. When urine can't get out, toxins and electrolytes build up in the blood. The Cornell Feline Health Center calls urethral obstruction an absolute emergency and notes that a fully blocked cat can die in as little as a day or two without treatment.</p>

<p>Early on, a blocked cat can look like a cat with an ordinary bladder flare-up: in and out of the box, squatting in odd places, licking at his genitals, crying while trying to pee. As time passes he becomes more distressed. If you aren't sure whether anything is coming out, assume it isn't and call a vet. The same goes for a cat of either sex who is in obvious pain while urinating or passing blood and seems unwell.</p>

<h2>Male Cat Marking vs. Litter Box Problem: Which One Are You Dealing With?</h2>

<p>This is the fork in the road, and it's worth a few days of watching before you change anything. Cornell puts it simply: cats don't squat to spray the way they do to urinate.</p>

<h3>Signs your cat is spraying (marking)</h3>

<p>A spraying cat backs up to an upright surface such as a wall, the side of a sofa, a door frame or curtains. He stands tall with his tail straight up, often quivering, sometimes treading with his back feet, and sends a small stream of urine backwards. The ASPCA notes that sprayed urine is usually a smaller amount than a normal void and often smells stronger, because it carries extra scent chemicals meant for other cats. Marks tend to appear in the same few spots, often near doors, windows or new objects. Crucially, a marking cat usually <em>keeps using the litter box</em> for normal urination, so urine in the box doesn't rule marking out.</p>

<p>Marking isn't only a tomcat habit. It's most common in unneutered males, but VCA and Cornell both note that around 10% of neutered males and 5% of spayed females still spray.</p>

<h3>Signs your cat is avoiding the box (inappropriate elimination)</h3>

<p>A cat with a litter box problem squats and leaves a normal-sized puddle on a flat surface: a rug, bath mat, bed or pile of laundry, or sometimes the bathtub or tile floor. She may stop using the box entirely or only use it some of the time.</p>

<p>If you have several cats and don't know who's responsible, ask your vet about fluorescein, a harmless dye given by mouth that makes urine glow under ultraviolet light. Temporarily separating cats one at a time also works.</p>

<h2>Why Is My Cat Peeing Outside the Litter Box All of a Sudden? Rule Out Medical Causes First</h2>

<p>Whichever behavior you see, the next step is a vet visit. The ASPCA and VCA both say medical problems should be ruled out before anything is treated as purely behavioral, and Cornell adds that cats with medical conditions don't always act sick. Expect a physical exam and a urinalysis, possibly followed by bloodwork, X-rays, ultrasound or a urine culture.</p>

<h3>Feline idiopathic cystitis (FIC)</h3>

<p>Cornell names FIC the most common diagnosis in cats with lower urinary tract signs. "Idiopathic" means the cause isn't fully known. The bladder becomes inflamed and painful, so the cat feels a frequent, urgent need to go, passes small amounts, and may have blood in the urine. Stress seems to play an important role. Episodes often settle within a couple of weeks but tend to come back, so management focuses on a calm, predictable home.</p>

<h3>Bladder stones and crystals</h3>

<p>Mineral stones and crystals can irritate the bladder or lodge in the urethra. The two most common types in cats are struvite and calcium oxalate. Some struvite stones can be dissolved with a prescription diet; calcium oxalate stones can't, and often need to be flushed out or surgically removed.</p>

<h3>Urinary tract infection</h3>

<p>Bacterial infections can cause the same frequent, uncomfortable trips to the box. But because FIC is the more common diagnosis, much of what owners assume is a "cat UTI" turns out to be something else, which is why the urinalysis matters. Our <a href="/tools/cat-uti-symptoms-guide/">cat UTI symptoms guide</a> covers how these are diagnosed, and our post on <a href="/blog/dog-uti-signs-symptoms/">signs of a UTI in dogs</a> explains why the picture in dogs is different.</p>

<h3>Kidney disease, diabetes and hyperthyroidism</h3>

<p>Some conditions don't make urinating painful; they just make a cat produce a lot more of it. Cornell lists kidney disease, thyroid disease and diabetes mellitus as possible causes of litter box problems because they make cats drink and urinate more. If you're scooping more or bigger clumps than you used to, mention it to your vet. Our guides to <a href="/tools/cat-kidney-disease-guide/">kidney disease in cats</a>, <a href="/tools/cat-diabetes-guide/">feline diabetes</a> and <a href="/tools/cat-hyperthyroidism-guide/">hyperthyroidism</a> cover the other signs that tend to come with each.</p>

<h3>Arthritis and age-related changes</h3>

<p>An older cat may know exactly where the box is and still not make it. Stiff joints turn high-sided boxes, top-entry boxes and stairs into hard work, and VCA notes that many older cats won't go down to a basement box. A low-entry box on every floor often helps more than you'd expect. Our <a href="/tools/cat-arthritis-guide/">cat arthritis guide</a> covers the subtle signs of joint pain.</p>

<p>One point applies to every cause above. If peeing hurt for a while, a cat can learn to connect the box itself with pain. VCA and the ASPCA both note that avoidance can continue after the medical problem is treated, so a healed bladder doesn't always mean an instant return to the box.</p>

<h2>Cat Suddenly Not Using the Litter Box? Look Hard at the Box Itself</h2>

<p>Once your vet gives the all-clear, look at the setup through your cat's nose.</p>

<h3>Cleanliness</h3>

<p>The ASPCA recommends scooping at least once a day and doing a full litter change and wash with warm water and unscented soap or baking soda about once a week. Some cats refuse a box with any waste in it. Never clean the box or accidents with ammonia-based products. Urine contains ammonia, so the smell can draw a cat back to the same spot. Use an enzymatic cleaner made for pet urine instead.</p>

<h3>Litter type and depth</h3>

<p>Most cats prefer unscented, clumping litter with a fine to medium texture, about one to two inches deep, and many stick with whatever they used as kittens. If you switched brands or scents and the problem started soon after, that's your prime suspect. Try two or three boxes side by side with different litters and let your cat choose.</p>

<h3>The box and where it sits</h3>

<p>Covers and liners are for human convenience. Cornell notes that most cats prefer an open box that lets odors escape and gives a full view of the room. A box that's too small is another common reason for avoidance; the ASPCA suggests a large plastic storage container as an easy upgrade. Avoid spots next to a noisy washer or furnace, busy hallways, and dead-end corners where a cat could feel trapped, and keep food and water bowls well away from the box.</p>

<h3>How many litter boxes for multiple cats?</h3>

<p>The standard guidance from the ASPCA and Cornell is one box per cat, plus one extra, so three cats need at least four boxes. Placement matters as much as the number. Four boxes lined up in one room work like one big box. Spread them across rooms, and floors if you have them, so no single cat controls access to all of them.</p>

<h2>What Changed? Stress, Territory and Other Animals</h2>

<p>If your cat is healthy and the box is spotless and well placed, look at what has changed in the cat's world. This is also the main driver of spraying in neutered cats. The ASPCA's list of triggers is broad: a new baby, someone moving in or out, a new dog or cat, renovations, a change in work hours, even an unusually large shopping bag.</p>

<p>Conflict between cats is one of the most common triggers, and it isn't always obvious fighting. One cat sitting near the box or blocking a hallway can be enough to send another cat elsewhere. Outdoor cats count too. A neighborhood cat passing the patio door is a classic reason for an indoor cat to start spraying right next to it.</p>

<p>Things that help:</p>

<ul>
<li>Have your cat neutered or spayed if they aren't already. It removes the mating motive for marking and can sometimes stop it completely. See our <a href="/tools/cat-spay-neuter-guide/">cat spay and neuter guide</a>.</li>
<li>Give each cat its own food, water, scratching posts and resting spots, spread around the home.</li>
<li>Add vertical space such as shelves, window perches and cat trees, so cats can keep their distance.</li>
<li>Block your cat's view of outdoor cats at the windows where marking happens.</li>
<li>Keep routines, including feeding times, consistent. Cornell notes that diet and schedule changes can trigger FIC flares too.</li>
<li>Consider a synthetic feline pheromone diffuser near problem areas, which the ASPCA says has shown some effect in relieving stress.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/tools/cat-stress-signs-guide/">cat stress signs guide</a> covers the quieter signs that often come first, such as over-grooming and hiding. If marking continues despite these changes, a veterinary behaviorist can help, and some cats benefit from anti-anxiety medication prescribed by a vet.</p>

<h2>What Not to Do</h2>

<p>Punishment reliably makes this worse. Don't rub a cat's nose in urine, scold her, or carry her to the box. Don't shut her in a small room with the box for weeks without tackling the cause. And it isn't spite. Cats don't pee to get back at you. Something is wrong, and this is how they're telling you.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Why is my cat peeing outside the litter box all of a sudden?</h3>
<p>A sudden change in a cat who has always used the box points to a medical problem first. Common causes include bladder inflammation (FIC), stones, infection, kidney disease, diabetes and arthritis. If your vet rules those out, look at recent changes to the litter, box, location or household. A male cat who is straining needs to be seen immediately.</p>

<h3>How can I tell if my cat is spraying or peeing?</h3>
<p>A spraying cat stands upright and sends a small amount of urine onto a vertical surface like a wall or furniture, often with a quivering tail. A cat peeing outside the box squats and leaves a normal-sized puddle on a flat surface. Cats who spray usually still use the box for normal urination.</p>

<h3>Do neutered male cats still spray?</h3>
<p>Some do. Neutering often reduces or stops spraying, but VCA and Cornell note that around 10% of neutered males and 5% of spayed females continue, usually because of stress or conflict with other cats.</p>

<h3>How many litter boxes do I need for multiple cats?</h3>
<p>One per cat plus one extra, so two cats need three boxes. Spread them across different rooms or floors so no single cat can block access to all of them.</p>

<h3>Is a cat straining to pee an emergency?</h3>
<p>It can be. If a cat, particularly a male, is straining repeatedly and producing little or no urine, he may have a urethral obstruction. That is life-threatening and needs a vet or emergency vet immediately, not a wait-and-see approach.</p>

